===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒɛn.tɹɪ.fɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|urban studies}} The [[renewal]] and [[rebuilding]] that accompanies the [[influx]] of [[middle class]] or [[affluent]] people into [[deteriorating]] areas and often [[displace]]s earlier, usually [[poor]]er, [[resident]]s; any example of such a process.
#: {{hyper|en|embourgeoisement}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jeremy Bugler|title=Tomorrow's London|accessdate=2016-09-12|newspaper=The Observer|location=London|date=1973-04-08|page=13|passage=Labour's manifesto contains the wild promise of 'war on the private landlord,' but this may conceal a real determination to use the powers of compulsory purchase to prevent the existing residents of places like North Kensington being driven out by the twin forces of ''''gentrification'''' and development.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|writer={{w|Spike Lee}}|title={{w|Do the Right Thing}}|year=1989|role=Buggin' Out|actor=Giancarlo Esposito|passage=Who told you to buy a brownstone on ''my'' block, in ''my'' neighborhood, on ''my'' side of the street? Yo, what you wanna live in a Black neighborhood for, anyway? Man, motherfuck '''gentrification'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Arthur C. Nelson et al.|title=The Social Impacts of Urban Containment|isbn=9781409487593|publisher=Ashgate|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=mr75LFD6r4kC&pg=PA71&dq=%22gentrification%22%7C%22gentrifications%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwipzrqklbvPAhUFG5QKHWgGCXQQ6AEIygIwNw#v=onepage&q=%22gentrification%22%7C%22gentrifications%22&f=false|page=71|passage=In particular, the focus is on property value changes and '''gentrification''' in Portland that are often attributed to urban growth and containment policies within the state.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Jerry Carrier|title=The Making of the Slave Class|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=P0r2yfJItskC&pg=PA177&dq=%22gentrification%22%7C%22gentrifications%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwipzrqklbvPAhUFG5QKHWgGCXQQ6AEI5AIwOw#v=onepage&q=%22gentrification%22%7C%22gentrifications%22&f=false|page=177|isbn=9780875867700|publisher=Algora|passage=I went to a large national forum not that long ago where about six hundred local government officials, nonprofit and neighborhood development corporation staff met to discuss the '''gentrification''' of urban neighborhoods. The irony is that the forum quickly shifted to discussions on race as it frequently does when '''gentrification''' is discussed.}}

